2. **major — proxyRec never resets a claimed record**: stale fields after wrap and sticky woke. Fix: zero the record in proxyRec on claim... wait — but proxyRec is called on EVERY event (doorbell, parts, flag) — zeroing on every event call would erase previously-written fields of the same record! The claim should zero only when CLAIMING (first event for this seq). How to detect? Compare `rec.seq != q` → new claim → zero the record first (then write seq). Events for the same q (rec.seq == q) just write their field. But wraparound: a new epoch record with the same slot has rec.seq != q (old q') → zero + claim ✓. Same-q repeated events skip the zero ✓. But subtle: the record is claimed at the FIRST event (maybe the flag before the doorbell). Later events find rec.seq == q → just write their field ✓.

Edge: what about `rec.flags |= 1` (woke) — with the zero-on-claim, woke sets on claim only... but woke should set on the FIRST event (claim) ✓ and subsequent events of the same record don't clear it ✓. But currently the woke handling is in proxyRec before seq write. With zero-on-claim: on claim, zero → then if woke: flags |= 1. On non-claim events: don't touch flags. But the current code sets `rec.flags |= 1` on EVERY proxyRec call while tr.woke... after first call tr.woke=false so only once ✓. Restructure:

```zig
fn proxyRec(r: *Ring, q: u64) ?*ring_trace.ProxyRec {
    if (!r.tr.on or !ring_trace.sampled(q, r.tr.n)) return null;
    const rec: *ring_trace.ProxyRec = @ptrCast(@alignCast(&r.tr.host[...] ));
    if (rec.seq != q) { // a new claim: the slot's old record goes (wraparound keeps it fresh)
        rec.* = .{};
        rec.seq = q;
        if (r.tr.woke) { rec.flags = 1; r.tr.woke = false; }
        r.traceNote(q);
    }
    return rec;
}
```
Hmm — but "rec.seq = q" then events write fields... the drain's completeness protocol relies on fields written before the progress marks ✓ unchanged. But there's a subtle hazard with zero-then-claim racing the DRAIN reader: the drain reads records below settle — for q < settle the record was claimed long ago and all fields written; the zero-then-write only happens for NEW claims (q' > settle... could a new claim for a record slot happen while the drain reads an OLD record in that slot that is ≤ settle but not yet emitted?? YES — wraparound: old record o (seq ≤ settle, not yet emitted because the watermark... wait — the watermark: emitted records advance proxy_mark. An unemitted record (seq ≤ settle but > mark) could be overwritten by a new claim before the drain emits it! That's the "drops" case — the drain counts it as dropped (expected − kept) since its seq changed ✓. But the DRAIN READ could see a HALF-ZEROED record (mid claim) with the NEW seq q' — if q' > settle the drain skips it ✓ (seq filter). If q' ≤ settle... q' is new (recent) — settle ≥ q' only if the flag for q' already published — then the record is complete... but the drain might read it MID-CLAIM (zeroed, seq already = q' but fields not yet written)! The seq filter passes (q' ≤ settle) and fields are zeros/torn! Hmm — when can this happen? settle = min(max_doorbell, max_flag) — q' ≤ settle requires max_doorbell ≥ q' — the doorbell event for q' happened (its record fully claimed + doorbell+last_post written). The claim (zero + seq) happened BEFORE the doorbell event write... the drain reads after acquiring settle... the acquire of max_doorbell (release store AFTER rec writes) — the drain's reads of the record fields happen-after the release ✓ SO: if the drain sees settle ≥ q', all writes before that release (the record's claim + doorbell + last_post) are visible. But the FLAG write might come later... for q' ≤ settle: max_flag ≥ q' too — flag write + release visible ✓. And PARTS writes precede the flag write ✓. So all fields visible. The mid-claim zero can only be observed for q' > settle (skipped). ✓ SAFE.

